<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="6d0eed4f">&lt;p&gt;
    The key differences between a Request for Information (RFI) and a Request for Proposal (RFP) is the timing and level of
    detail requested from suppliers to provide. Stakeholders submit a Request for Information (RFI) to acquire information
    about a suppliers capabilities. An RFI is not a contractual solicitation and is not intended to replace a Request for
    Proposal (RFP). An RFI is used to ask questions that facilitate understanding and to differentiate between a variety of
    solutions. The written information submitted by the supplier is used to identify those that warrant further evaluation,
    typically performed in an RFP. An RFP is submitted to procure a package based on fulfillment of specified requirements
    to implement a solution. An RFP is typically more detailed and usually asks for suppliers to provide an implementation
    plan, timeline and pricing information.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</attribute>
